/* ======================= DISK & SYSTEM ======================= */
lesson('df', {
  n: 'df', s: 'Shows how much space is used and free on each mounted filesystem.',
  an: 'Checking the fuel gauge of every tank in a fleet: how big each tank is, how full, how much is left.',
  nm: '<b>d</b>isk <b>f</b>ree.',
  sx: 'df [OPTIONS] [FILE|MOUNTPOINT]...', p: [['df', 'cmd', 'The command.', true], ['[OPTIONS]', 'opt', '-h human sizes, -T show type, -i inodes.', false], ['[FILE]...', 'arg', 'Show only the filesystem containing this path.', false]],
  ex1: 'df -h',
  sc: [['openat /proc/self/mountinfo', 'reads the list of mounted filesystems', 'openat(AT_FDCWD, "/proc/self/mountinfo", O_RDONLY) = 3'], ['statfs (×N)', 'asks each filesystem for total/free/available blocks and inodes', 'statfs("/", {f_bsize=4096, f_blocks=12868767, f_bfree=7393641, f_bavail=6735481, …}) = 0'], ['write', 'prints the table', 'write(1, "Filesystem  Size  Used Avail Use% Mounted on\\n…", 320) = 320']],
  ch: 'Nothing.', out: 'Filesystem, Size, Used, Avail, Use%, Mounted on.', ec: '0 (1 if some filesystem couldn\'t be read).',
  f: [['-h', '--human-readable', 'Sizes in K/M/G (powers of 1024).', 'df -h', true], ['-T', '--print-type', 'Show filesystem type (ext4, tmpfs…).', 'df -hT', true], ['-i', '--inodes', 'Inode usage instead of blocks.', 'df -i', true], ['-H', '--si', 'Powers of 1000.', 'df -H', false], ['-x TYPE', '', 'Exclude a type.', 'df -h -x tmpfs', false]],
  e: [['df -h', 'Filesystem      Size  Used Avail Use% Mounted on\n/dev/sda1        49G   21G   28G  43% /', 'Overview.'], ['df -hT', '', 'With filesystem types.'], ['df -h .', '', 'Only the filesystem holding the current directory.'], ['df -i', '', 'Inodes: you can run out of them with millions of tiny files!'], ['sudo mount /dev/sdb1 /mnt; df -h', '', 'A newly mounted USB stick appears.']],
  u: `<p>df asks each <b>mounted filesystem</b> how many blocks it has in total and free. Note <b>Used + Avail &lt; Size</b> on ext4: about 5% is reserved for root so the system can still work when users fill the disk.</p><p>A full disk breaks things in odd ways (failed writes, services crashing). Common culprits: logs, caches, Docker images. Use <code>du</code> to find which directory is big. If df says full but du can't find the files, a process may hold a deleted file open (<code>lsof +L1</code>).</p>`,
  adv: `<p>df calls <code>statfs(2)</code>/<code>statvfs</code>: f_blocks (total), f_bfree (free incl. reserved), f_bavail (free for unprivileged users), f_files/f_ffree (inodes). Pseudo-filesystems (proc, sysfs, cgroup) are hidden unless -a. tmpfs sizes are limits on RAM usage, not disk.</p>`,
  mi: [['df (raw numbers)', '1K-blocks are hard to read.', 'df -h'], ['Expecting df to show folder sizes', 'It shows whole filesystems.', 'du -sh folder'], ['“Disk full” but df -h shows space', 'You may be out of INODES.', 'df -i']],
  safe: 'Read-only.',
  r: [['du', 'df = whole filesystem; du = specific folders.'], ['lsblk', 'Disks and partitions, mounted or not.'], ['mount', 'What is mounted where.']],
  cs: ['<code>df -h</code> space per filesystem', '<code>df -h .</code> just this one', '<code>df -i</code> inode usage', '~5% reserved for root on ext4', 'Find culprits with du'],
  q: [['Which shows sizes like 28G?', ['df -k', 'df -h', 'df -i', 'df -T'], 1, 'Human-readable.'], ['df shows 100% but you deleted a big log. Why might space not return?', ['df caches', 'A process still has it open', 'Trash', 'Reserved blocks'], 1, 'Space is freed when the last handle closes.'], ['What does df -i show?', ['Inode usage', 'Installed packages', 'Interfaces', 'IOPS'], 0, 'Inodes can run out too.']],
  c: { t: 'Show disk usage of all filesystems in human-readable sizes.', try: 'df -h', chk: ctx => okRun(ctx, 'df') && /-\w*h/.test(ctx.line) },
});
lesson('du', {
  n: 'du', s: 'Measures how much disk space files and directories use.',
  an: 'Weighing each moving box to find out which room filled up the truck.',
  nm: '<b>d</b>isk <b>u</b>sage.',
  sx: 'du [OPTIONS] [FILE|DIR]...', p: [['du', 'cmd', 'The command.', true], ['[OPTIONS]', 'opt', '-s summary, -h human, -a all files, -d depth, -c total.', false], ['[FILE|DIR]...', 'arg', 'What to measure (default .).', false]],
  ex1: 'du -sh docs logs',
  sc: [['openat/getdents64', 'walks the directory tree', 'getdents64(3, /* 5 entries */, 32768) = 168'], ['newfstatat (×N)', 'reads st_blocks (allocated size) of every file', 'newfstatat(3, "app.log", {…, st_blocks=4920, …}, AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW) = 0'], ['write', 'prints totals', 'write(1, "16K\\tdocs\\n4.0M\\tlogs\\n", 20) = 20']],
  ch: 'Nothing.', out: '<code>SIZE&lt;TAB&gt;PATH</code> lines; by default every subdirectory, in KiB.', ec: '0, 1 if some directories couldn\'t be read.',
  f: [['-s', '--summarize', 'One total per argument.', 'du -s docs', true], ['-h', '--human-readable', 'K/M/G.', 'du -sh *', true], ['-a', '--all', 'Include files, not just dirs.', 'du -ah docs', false], ['-d N', '--max-depth=N', 'Limit depth.', 'du -h -d 1 /var', true], ['-c', '--total', 'Grand total line.', 'du -shc *', false], ['-x', '--one-file-system', 'Don\'t cross into other mounts.', 'du -shx /', false]],
  e: [['du -sh docs logs', '16K\tdocs\n4.0M\tlogs', 'Size of two folders.'], ['du -h -d 1', '', 'Each top-level folder here.'], ['du -sh * | sort -h', '', 'Smallest to biggest — find the space hog.'], ['du -ah docs', '', 'Files too.'], ['sudo du -sh /var/log', '', 'System folders often need sudo.']],
  u: `<p>du adds up the <b>allocated blocks</b> of files, not their apparent length: a 1-byte file still occupies one 4 KiB block, and sparse files may use less than their size. That's why du and ls -l can disagree (<code>du --apparent-size</code> gives the ls-like number).</p><p>Workflow for a full disk: <code>df -h</code> (which filesystem?) → <code>du -h -d 1 /path | sort -h</code> (which folder?) → drill down.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>du sums <code>st_blocks × 512</code>; hard-linked files are counted once (it remembers dev/inode pairs). It can't see files that were deleted but are still open — those show in df but not du.</p>`,
  mi: [['du /', 'Prints every directory on the system for minutes.', 'sudo du -h -d 1 -x / | sort -h'], ['du -h | sort -n', '“2.0M” sorts before “900K”.', 'du -h | sort -h'], ['Comparing du and ls -l sizes', 'Blocks vs bytes differ.', 'du --apparent-size -h']],
  safe: 'Read-only.',
  r: [['df', 'Free space per filesystem.'], ['ls', 'ls -lh for single files.'], ['find', 'find -size +100M to list big files.']],
  cs: ['<code>du -sh dir</code> total of a folder', '<code>du -h -d 1 | sort -h</code> biggest subfolders', 'Counts allocated blocks', '<code>-x</code> stay on one filesystem', 'df → which disk, du → which folder'],
  q: [['Which gives one line per argument?', ['-a', '-s', '-c', '-d 0 only'], 1, '-s = summarize (same as -d 0).'], ['Why might a 1-byte file show 4.0K in du?', ['Bug', 'Space is allocated in blocks', 'Metadata', 'Compression'], 1, 'Minimum allocation is a block.'], ['How to sort du -h output correctly?', ['sort -n', 'sort -h', 'sort -r', 'sort -k1'], 1, '-h understands K/M/G.']],
  c: { t: 'Find out how much space the <code>logs</code> folder uses, human-readable.', try: 'du -sh logs', chk: ctx => okRun(ctx, 'du') && ctx.line.includes('logs') && /h/.test(ctx.line) },
});
lesson('free', {
  n: 'free', s: 'Shows how much RAM and swap is used and available.',
  an: 'Looking at a desk: some space has your active work (used), some holds handy reference books you can clear away instantly (cache), the rest is empty.',
  nm: '<b>free</b> (memory).',
  sx: 'free [-h|-m|-g] [-s SECS]', p: [['free', 'cmd', 'The command.', true], ['[-h|-m|-g]', 'opt', 'Units: human, MiB, GiB.', false], ['[-s SECS]', 'opt', 'Repeat every SECS seconds.', false]],
  ex1: 'free -h',
  sc: [['openat/read /proc/meminfo', 'everything comes from the kernel\'s memory statistics', 'read(3, "MemTotal:  8041512 kB\\nMemFree: …", 4096) = 1452'], ['write', 'prints the table', 'write(1, "               total        used …", 210) = 210']],
  ch: 'Nothing.', out: 'Rows Mem and Swap; columns total, used, free, shared, buff/cache, available.', ec: '0.',
  f: [['-h', '--human', 'Human units (Gi, Mi).', 'free -h', true], ['-m / -g', '', 'MiB / GiB.', 'free -m', false], ['-s N', '', 'Refresh every N seconds.', 'free -h -s 2', false], ['-t', '', 'Add a total row.', 'free -ht', false]],
  e: [['free -h', '               total   used   free  shared  buff/cache  available\nMem:           7.7Gi  2.4Gi  3.0Gi   51Mi       2.3Gi      5.3Gi', 'Overview.'], ['free -m', '', 'In MiB.'], ['cat /proc/meminfo | head -5', '', 'The raw source.'], ['top -b -n 1 | head -5', '', 'top shows the same numbers.'], ['vmstat 1 3', '', '(real Linux) memory/swap activity over time.']],
  u: `<p><b>Don't panic about low “free”.</b> Linux uses spare RAM as a disk cache (<b>buff/cache</b>) to speed things up, and gives it back instantly when programs need it. The number that matters is <b>available</b>: how much programs could get without swapping.</p><p><b>Swap</b> is disk space used as overflow memory. Some swap use is normal; constant swapping in and out makes the system crawl.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>available = MemFree + reclaimable page cache + reclaimable slab − low watermarks (kernel's own estimate, MemAvailable). When memory runs out, the OOM killer picks a victim by <code>oom_score</code> (/proc/PID/oom_score). Cgroups can set per-container limits (memory.max).</p>`,
  mi: [['Seeing low “free” and buying RAM', 'Cache makes free look small.', 'Look at “available”'], ['free in a container', 'Often shows the HOST\'s memory, not the container limit.', 'cat /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.max'], ['Disabling swap to “speed up”', 'Can cause OOM kills under pressure.', 'Tune vm.swappiness instead']],
  safe: 'Read-only.',
  r: [['top', 'Memory per process.'], ['proc', '/proc/meminfo is the source.'], ['df', 'Disk space, not memory.']],
  cs: ['<code>free -h</code> RAM & swap', 'Look at <b>available</b>, not free', 'buff/cache = reusable disk cache', 'Swap = overflow on disk', 'Source: /proc/meminfo'],
  q: [['Which column best shows usable memory?', ['free', 'used', 'available', 'shared'], 2, 'Includes reclaimable cache.'], ['What is buff/cache?', ['Wasted RAM', 'Disk cache the kernel can reclaim', 'Swap', 'GPU memory'], 1, 'Reclaimed on demand.'], ['Where does free read its data?', ['/etc/meminfo', '/proc/meminfo', '/dev/mem', 'dmesg'], 1, '/proc/meminfo.']],
  c: { t: 'Check memory usage in human-readable units.', try: 'free -h', chk: ctx => okRun(ctx, 'free') },
});
lesson('uname', {
  n: 'uname', s: 'Prints system information: kernel name, version, hostname, CPU architecture.',
  an: 'Reading the manufacturer\'s plate on a machine: model, serial, build version.',
  nm: '<b>u</b>nix <b>name</b>.',
  sx: 'uname [-a|-s|-n|-r|-v|-m|-o]', p: [['uname', 'cmd', 'The command.', true], ['[OPTIONS]', 'opt', 'Which fields; -a = all.', false]],
  ex1: 'uname -a',
  sc: [['uname', 'one system call fills a struct with sysname, nodename, release, version, machine', 'uname({sysname="Linux", nodename="linux", release="6.8.0-49-generic", …}) = 0'], ['write', 'prints the requested fields', 'write(1, "Linux linux 6.8.0-49-generic …", 104) = 104']],
  ch: 'Nothing.', out: 'Space-separated fields.', ec: '0.',
  f: [['-a', '--all', 'Everything.', 'uname -a', true], ['-r', '--kernel-release', 'Kernel version.', 'uname -r', true], ['-m', '--machine', 'Hardware architecture (x86_64, aarch64).', 'uname -m', true], ['-n', '--nodename', 'Hostname.', 'uname -n', false], ['-s', '--kernel-name', 'Kernel name (default).', 'uname -s', false], ['-o', '--operating-system', 'OS (GNU/Linux).', 'uname -o', false]],
  e: [['uname', 'Linux', 'Kernel name.'], ['uname -r', '6.8.0-49-generic', 'Kernel version — useful when installing drivers/headers.'], ['uname -m', 'x86_64', 'CPU architecture — pick the right download.'], ['uname -a', 'Linux linux 6.8.0-49-generic #49-Ubuntu …', 'All at once.'], ['cat /etc/os-release', 'PRETTY_NAME="Ubuntu 24.04.1 LTS"…', 'The DISTRIBUTION version (uname shows the kernel).']],
  u: `<p><b>Kernel vs distribution:</b> uname reports the kernel (Linux 6.8). The distribution (Ubuntu 24.04, Fedora 41…) is in <code>/etc/os-release</code> or <code>lsb_release -a</code>. Architecture matters when downloading software: x86_64 (amd64) vs aarch64 (arm64).</p>`,
  adv: `<p><code>uname(2)</code> copies <code>struct new_utsname</code> from the current UTS namespace — inside a container you may see a different hostname (UTS namespace) but always the host's kernel release, because containers share the kernel.</p>`,
  mi: [['uname -v for the distro version', 'Shows the kernel build string.', 'cat /etc/os-release'], ['Assuming arm64 binaries run on x86_64', 'Exec format error.', 'Check uname -m first'], ['uname inside Docker to learn the image OS', 'Shows the host kernel.', 'cat /etc/os-release inside the container']],
  safe: 'Read-only.',
  r: [['uptime', 'How long the kernel has been running.'], ['hostname', 'Show/change the host name.']],
  cs: ['<code>uname -a</code> everything', '<code>uname -r</code> kernel version', '<code>uname -m</code> architecture', 'Distro: /etc/os-release', 'Containers share the host kernel'],
  q: [['Which shows the kernel version?', ['uname -m', 'uname -r', 'uname -n', 'uname -o'], 1, '-r = release.'], ['Where is the distribution name?', ['uname -d', '/etc/os-release', '/proc/version only', 'uname -s'], 1, '/etc/os-release.'], ['uname -m prints x86_64. What does it mean?', ['Memory size', '64-bit Intel/AMD CPU architecture', 'Kernel', 'Hostname'], 1, 'Machine hardware name.']],
  c: { t: 'Find out the kernel release and CPU architecture.', try: 'uname -a', chk: ctx => okRun(ctx, 'uname') && /-\w*[arm]/.test(ctx.line) },
});
lesson('uptime', {
  n: 'uptime', s: 'Shows how long the system has been running, logged-in users, and the load average.',
  an: 'The odometer and engine-load gauge of a car in one glance.',
  nm: '<b>uptime</b> — time since boot.',
  sx: 'uptime [-p|-s]', p: [['uptime', 'cmd', 'The command.', true], ['[-p|-s]', 'opt', '-p pretty, -s since when.', false]],
  ex1: 'uptime',
  sc: [['read /proc/uptime', 'seconds since boot', 'read(3, "15342.51 58010.12\\n", 8191) = 18'], ['read /proc/loadavg', 'load averages', 'read(4, "0.42 0.37 0.31 2/412 2141\\n", 8191) = 26'], ['read /run/utmp', 'logged-in users', 'openat(AT_FDCWD, "/run/utmp", O_RDONLY|O_CLOEXEC) = 5']],
  ch: 'Nothing.', out: '<code> 09:34:12 up 4:15, 1 user, load average: 0.42, 0.37, 0.31</code>', ec: '0.',
  f: [['-p', '--pretty', 'up 4 hours, 15 minutes.', 'uptime -p', true], ['-s', '--since', 'Boot date/time.', 'uptime -s', true]],
  e: [['uptime', ' 09:34:12 up  4:15,  1 user,  load average: 0.42, 0.37, 0.31', 'Full line.'], ['uptime -p', 'up 4 hours, 15 minutes', 'Readable.'], ['uptime -s', '2026-11-18 05:19:03', 'When it booted.'], ['cat /proc/loadavg', '0.42 0.37 0.31 2/412 2141', 'Raw: also running/total tasks and last PID.'], ['nproc', '', '(real Linux) number of CPUs to compare load against.']],
  u: `<p><b>Load average</b> = average number of tasks that were running or waiting (for CPU, or stuck in uninterruptible disk I/O) over the last 1, 5 and 15 minutes. Compare with the number of CPU cores: on 4 cores, load 4 means fully busy; 8 means tasks are queueing. Rising 1-min vs 15-min shows a trend.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>The kernel samples nr_running + nr_uninterruptible every 5 s and keeps exponentially-damped moving averages (fixed-point, see <code>calc_global_load</code>). PSI (<code>/proc/pressure/cpu|memory|io</code>) gives more precise “time stalled” metrics on modern kernels.</p>`,
  mi: [['Load 2.0 = 200% CPU?', 'Load counts tasks, not percent; on 4 cores it\'s 50% busy.', 'Compare with nproc'], ['High load but idle CPU', 'Tasks are waiting on disk (D state).', 'Check iowait in top'], ['Rebooting to “fix” uptime', 'Long uptime is fine — but kernel security updates need a reboot.', 'Check needrestart']],
  safe: 'Read-only.',
  r: [['top', 'Same header plus processes.'], ['free', 'Memory overview.'], ['uname', 'Kernel info.']],
  cs: ['<code>uptime</code> time since boot + load', '<code>uptime -p</code> pretty', 'Load = runnable/waiting tasks (1/5/15 min)', 'Compare load with number of cores', 'Source: /proc/uptime, /proc/loadavg'],
  q: [['Load average 4.0 on a 2-core machine means…', ['idle', 'about 2 tasks waiting per core — overloaded', '4% CPU', 'nothing'], 1, 'Load > cores means queueing.'], ['Which shows the boot time?', ['uptime -b', 'uptime -s', 'uptime -t', 'uptime -r'], 1, '-s = since.'], ['The three numbers are averages over…', ['1/5/15 minutes', '1/5/15 seconds', 'CPU 1/2/3', 'last 3 boots'], 0, '1, 5 and 15 minutes.']],
  c: { t: 'Show how long the system has been up, in pretty format.', try: 'uptime -p', chk: ctx => okRun(ctx, 'uptime') },
});

/* ======================= NETWORKING ======================= */
lesson('ping', {
  n: 'ping', s: 'Checks whether another machine is reachable and measures the round-trip time.',
  an: 'Shouting “Marco!” across a lake and timing how long until you hear “Polo!”.',
  nm: 'Named after the “ping” of sonar.',
  sx: 'ping [-c COUNT] [-i SECS] HOST', p: [['ping', 'cmd', 'The command.', true], ['[-c COUNT]', 'opt', 'Number of packets (otherwise runs until Ctrl+C).', false], ['HOST', 'arg', 'Hostname or IP address.', true]],
  ex1: 'ping -c 4 google.com',
  sc: [['(DNS lookup)', 'resolves google.com to an IP address', 'connect(3, {sa_family=AF_INET, sin_port=htons(53), sin_addr=inet_addr("127.0.0.53")}, 16) = 0'], ['socket', 'opens an ICMP socket', 'socket(AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, IPPROTO_ICMP) = 4'], ['sendto', 'sends an ICMP Echo Request', 'sendto(4, "\\10\\0…", 64, 0, {sin_addr=inet_addr("142.250.185.78")}, 16) = 64'], ['recvmsg', 'waits for the Echo Reply and timestamps it', 'recvmsg(4, {…}, 0) = 64']],
  ch: 'Nothing.', out: 'One line per reply with icmp_seq, ttl and time, then statistics (loss, min/avg/max).', ec: '0 = got replies, 1 = no replies, 2 = other error (e.g. unknown host).',
  f: [['-c N', '', 'Send N packets then stop.', 'ping -c 4 host', true], ['-i S', '', 'Interval between packets.', 'ping -i 0.2 host', false], ['-W S', '', 'Timeout for each reply.', 'ping -W 1 host', false], ['-q', '', 'Only the summary.', 'ping -q -c 10 host', false], ['-4 / -6', '', 'Force IPv4 / IPv6.', 'ping -6 host', false]],
  e: [['ping -c 4 google.com', 'PING google.com (142.250.185.78) …\n64 bytes from …: icmp_seq=1 ttl=61 time=14.1 ms', 'Internet reachable.'], ['ping -c 2 web01', '', 'A server on the local network (sub-millisecond).'], ['ping -c 3 web02', '100% packet loss', 'web02 is down (or drops ICMP).'], ['ping -c 2 nosuchhost', 'ping: nosuchhost: Name or service not known', 'DNS failure, not a network failure.'], ['ping -c 2 8.8.8.8', '', 'IP directly: skips DNS — good for isolating DNS problems.']],
  u: `<p>ping sends <b>ICMP Echo Request</b> packets; the target answers with <b>Echo Reply</b>. <b>time</b> = round trip; <b>ttl</b> = remaining hop budget (each router decrements it). Packet loss suggests congestion or a bad link.</p><p><b>Troubleshooting ladder:</b> ping your gateway → ping 8.8.8.8 (internet OK?) → ping google.com (DNS OK?). Note that many servers/firewalls block ICMP, so “no reply” doesn't always mean “down”.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>Modern Linux allows unprivileged ICMP via <code>SOCK_DGRAM, IPPROTO_ICMP</code> if your GID is within <code>net.ipv4.ping_group_range</code>; otherwise ping needs CAP_NET_RAW (file capability or setuid). Each packet carries a sequence number and timestamp; the kernel matches replies by the socket's ICMP id.</p>`,
  mi: [['ping host (and waiting forever)', 'Linux ping never stops on its own.', 'ping -c 4 host   (or Ctrl+C)'], ['“Server is down” because ping fails', 'ICMP may be blocked by a firewall.', 'curl -I http://host or nc -zv host 22'], ['Pinging a URL (ping https://x.com)', 'ping takes a host, not a URL.', 'ping x.com']],
  safe: 'Harmless (don\'t flood other people\'s machines with -f).',
  r: [['traceroute', 'WHERE along the path packets get lost/slow.'], ['curl', 'Test a web service (application level), not just reachability.'], ['dig', 'Test name resolution separately.']],
  cs: ['<code>ping -c 4 host</code>', 'time = round-trip latency', '100% loss = down OR ICMP blocked', 'Ping an IP to rule out DNS', 'Ctrl+C stops'],
  q: [['What protocol does ping use?', ['TCP', 'UDP', 'ICMP', 'HTTP'], 2, 'ICMP Echo Request/Reply.'], ['ping google.com fails but ping 8.8.8.8 works. Likely problem?', ['Cable', 'DNS', 'Firewall', 'CPU'], 1, 'Name resolution fails.'], ['How to send exactly 3 packets?', ['ping -n 3', 'ping -c 3', 'ping -3', 'ping --count3'], 1, '-c COUNT.']],
  c: { t: 'Ping <code>google.com</code> exactly 3 times.', try: 'ping -c 3 google.com', chk: ctx => okRun(ctx, 'ping') && /-c\s*3/.test(ctx.line) },
});
lesson('curl', {
  n: 'curl', s: 'Transfers data to or from a URL — the Swiss-army knife for testing web servers and APIs.',
  an: 'Sending a courier with a written request to an office and having them read the reply back to you word for word.',
  nm: '“<b>c</b>lient for <b>URL</b>s” (also “see URL”).',
  sx: 'curl [OPTIONS] URL', p: [['curl', 'cmd', 'The command.', true], ['[OPTIONS]', 'opt', '-I headers, -o file, -L follow redirects, -X method, -d data, -H header, -v verbose.', false], ['URL', 'arg', 'http(s)://host[:port]/path', true]],
  ex1: 'curl example.com',
  sc: [['(DNS)', 'resolve the host name', 'getaddrinfo("example.com") → 93.184.215.14'], ['socket + connect', 'TCP 3-way handshake to port 80/443', 'connect(5, {sin_port=htons(443), sin_addr=inet_addr("93.184.215.14")}, 16) = -1 EINPROGRESS'], ['(TLS handshake)', 'for https: certificate check + key exchange', ''], ['sendto', 'the HTTP request', 'sendto(5, "GET / HTTP/1.1\\r\\nHost: example.com\\r\\n…", 78, MSG_NOSIGNAL, NULL, 0) = 78'], ['recvfrom', 'the response headers and body', 'recvfrom(5, "HTTP/1.1 200 OK\\r\\n…", 102400, 0, NULL, NULL) = 1256'], ['write', 'prints the body to stdout', 'write(1, "<!doctype html>…", 1256) = 1256']],
  ch: 'Nothing locally unless -o/-O (save to file). Remote side effects possible with POST/PUT/DELETE.', out: 'The response BODY on stdout (headers with -i/-I, a progress meter on stderr when saving to a file).', ec: '0 = transfer OK (even for HTTP 404! use -f), 6 = couldn\'t resolve host, 7 = couldn\'t connect, 28 = timeout, 22 = HTTP error with -f.',
  f: [['-I', '--head', 'Fetch only headers.', 'curl -I https://google.com', true], ['-o FILE', '--output', 'Save body to FILE.', 'curl -o page.html example.com', true], ['-O', '--remote-name', 'Save with the remote file name.', 'curl -O https://x/file.tgz', false], ['-L', '--location', 'Follow redirects (301/302).', 'curl -L http://google.com', true], ['-s', '--silent', 'No progress meter / errors.', 'curl -s url', false], ['-v', '--verbose', 'Show the whole conversation.', 'curl -v example.com', true], ['-X M', '--request', 'HTTP method.', 'curl -X DELETE url', false], ['-d DATA', '--data', 'Send a POST body.', 'curl -d \'{"a":1}\' url', true], ['-H H', '--header', 'Add a header.', 'curl -H "Accept: application/json" url', false], ['-f', '--fail', 'Exit non-zero on HTTP errors.', 'curl -f url', false], ['-k', '--insecure', 'Skip TLS verification (testing only!).', 'curl -k https://self-signed', false]],
  e: [['curl example.com', '<!doctype html>\n<html>…', 'Print a web page.'], ['curl -I https://google.com', 'HTTP/2 200 OK\ncontent-type: text/html…', 'Only headers.'], ['curl -s https://api.example.com/users | jq .', '', 'Call a JSON API (jq after sudo apt install jq).'], ['curl -o page.html example.com', '', 'Download to a file.'], ['curl localhost', '<h1>Welcome to nginx!</h1>', 'Test your own web server.'], ['curl -v example.com 2>&1 | head -8', '', 'See request and response headers.']],
  u: `<p>An HTTP exchange: <b>DNS</b> (name → IP) → <b>TCP handshake</b> (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) → <b>TLS</b> for https → <b>request</b> (method, path, headers, optional body) → <b>response</b> (status code, headers, body).</p><p>Status codes: 2xx success (200 OK), 3xx redirect (301/302 → use -L), 4xx client error (404 not found, 403 forbidden), 5xx server error. curl prints the body to stdout by default — it's made for pipelines.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>curl is built on libcurl (also used by git, many apps). It supports HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2 (ALPN), HTTP/3 (QUIC), FTP, SCP/SFTP, SMTP… <code>--resolve host:443:IP</code> overrides DNS, <code>-w '%{http_code} %{time_total}\\n'</code> prints timing, <code>--cacert</code> sets trust. Certificates are verified against the system CA store (/etc/ssl/certs).</p>`,
  mi: [['curl http://google.com (prints “301 Moved”)', 'curl doesn\'t follow redirects by default.', 'curl -L http://google.com'], ['curl url > file.html then checking $? for 404', 'Exit code is 0 even on 404.', 'curl -f -o file.html url'], ['curl -k everywhere', 'Disables certificate checks → vulnerable to man-in-the-middle.', 'Fix the certificate / use --cacert'], ['curl https://x/install.sh | sudo bash', 'Runs unknown code as root.', 'Download, read, then run']],
  w: '<b>Piping downloaded scripts straight into a shell</b> (<code>curl … | bash</code>, especially with sudo) runs code you haven\'t read with your privileges. Download first, inspect, then run. Never use <code>-k</code>/<code>--insecure</code> outside testing.',
  r: [['wget', 'Download files (resume, recursive mirroring); curl = more protocols & API testing.'], ['ping', 'Basic reachability only.'], ['ss', 'See your own server\'s listening ports.'], ['dig', 'Debug the DNS step.']],
  cs: ['<code>curl url</code> print body', '<code>-I</code> headers · <code>-L</code> follow redirects', '<code>-o file</code> save · <code>-O</code> keep name', '<code>-X POST -d data -H header</code> APIs', 'Exit 0 even on 404 unless <code>-f</code>'],
  q: [['Which option shows only response headers?', ['-H', '-I', '-h', '-o'], 1, '-I (HEAD request).'], ['curl prints “301 Moved Permanently”. What helps?', ['-k', '-L', '-s', '-v'], 1, '-L follows redirects.'], ['Exit code 6 means…', ['404', 'Could not resolve host', 'Timeout', 'Success'], 1, 'DNS failure.']],
  c: { t: 'Fetch the home page of <code>example.com</code>, and also test your local web server at <code>localhost</code>.', try: 'curl example.com', steps: [['curl example.com', ctx => okRun(ctx, 'curl') && ctx.line.includes('example.com')], ['curl localhost', ctx => ran(ctx, 'curl') && ctx.line.includes('localhost')]] },
});
lesson('wget', {
  n: 'wget', s: 'Downloads files from the web and saves them to disk.',
  an: 'A delivery service: you give an address, it fetches the parcel and leaves it in your hallway (a file), retrying if the road is blocked.',
  nm: '<b>W</b>orld Wide <b>Web</b> <b>get</b>.',
  sx: 'wget [OPTIONS] URL...', p: [['wget', 'cmd', 'The command.', true], ['[OPTIONS]', 'opt', '-O name, -q quiet, -c continue, -r recursive.', false], ['URL...', 'arg', 'What to download.', true]],
  ex1: 'wget example.com',
  sc: [['(DNS + connect + TLS)', 'same network steps as curl', 'connect(3, {sin_port=htons(443), …}, 16) = 0'], ['sendto', 'HTTP GET', 'sendto(3, "GET / HTTP/1.1\\r\\n…", 125, 0, NULL, 0) = 125'], ['openat', 'creates the output file (index.html)', 'openat(AT_FDCWD, "index.html", O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_TRUNC, 0666) = 4'], ['recvfrom → write', 'streams the body into the file', 'write(4, "<!doctype html>…", 1256) = 1256']],
  ch: 'Creates a file in the current directory (adds .1, .2 if the name exists).', out: 'Progress information on stderr.', ec: '0 OK, 4 network failure, 8 server error response.',
  f: [['-O FILE', '', 'Save as FILE (- = stdout).', 'wget -O page.html url', true], ['-q', '--quiet', 'No output.', 'wget -q url', false], ['-c', '--continue', 'Resume a partial download.', 'wget -c big.iso', true], ['-r', '--recursive', 'Mirror links recursively.', 'wget -r -l 1 url', false], ['-b', '--background', 'Download in background.', 'wget -b url', false], ['--limit-rate', '', 'Bandwidth limit.', 'wget --limit-rate=1m url', false]],
  e: [['wget example.com', "Saving to: 'index.html'", 'Download the page.'], ['wget example.com', "Saving to: 'index.html.1'", 'Second time: doesn\'t overwrite, adds .1.'], ['wget -O home.html example.com', '', 'Choose the name.'], ['wget -qO- example.com | head -3', '', 'To stdout, like curl.'], ['wget -c https://x/big.iso', '', 'Resume after a broken connection (real network).']],
  u: `<p>wget is made for <b>downloading to files</b>: it saves by default, retries on failures, can resume (<code>-c</code>) and mirror whole sites (<code>-r</code>). curl is made for <b>talking to URLs</b>: prints to stdout, more protocols and options for APIs. Both are standard; pick wget for “just get this file”.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>-c uses the HTTP Range header (<code>Range: bytes=N-</code>) to fetch the rest. wget respects robots.txt during recursive downloads; <code>--mirror</code> = -r -N -l inf --no-remove-listing. HSTS state is cached in ~/.wget-hsts.</p>`,
  mi: [['wget url (expecting output on screen)', 'It writes a file instead.', 'wget -qO- url   (or curl url)'], ['wget -O file with several URLs', 'All are concatenated into one file.', 'Download them separately'], ['Interrupted large download → start over', 'Wastes bandwidth.', 'wget -c url']],
  safe: 'Creates files only; existing names are not overwritten (it appends .1) unless you use -O.',
  r: [['curl', 'Better for APIs, headers, uploads.'], ['scp', 'Download from a machine you can SSH into.'], ['rsync', 'Sync whole trees efficiently.']],
  cs: ['<code>wget url</code> → saves a file', '<code>-O name</code> choose the name', '<code>-c</code> resume', '<code>-qO-</code> print to stdout', 'Existing file? saves name.1'],
  q: [['Where does wget write by default?', ['stdout', 'a file in the current directory', '/tmp', '~/Downloads'], 1, 'Named after the URL path.'], ['How to resume a broken download?', ['-r', '-c', '-b', '-q'], 1, '-c continue.'], ['Main difference to curl?', ['wget can\'t do https', 'wget saves to files by default', 'curl can\'t download', 'none'], 1, 'curl prints to stdout.']],
  c: { t: 'Download <code>example.com</code> as a file named <code>home.html</code>.', try: 'wget -O home.html example.com', chk: ctx => has('home.html') },
});
lesson('ssh', {
  n: 'ssh', s: 'Logs into another machine over an encrypted connection, or runs a command there.',
  an: 'A secure video call into another office: everything you say and see is encrypted, and both sides check ID badges before talking.',
  nm: '<b>S</b>ecure <b>Sh</b>ell.',
  sx: 'ssh [OPTIONS] [USER@]HOST [COMMAND]', p: [['ssh', 'cmd', 'The client.', true], ['[OPTIONS]', 'opt', '-p port, -i key file, -v verbose, -L/-R tunnels.', false], ['[USER@]HOST', 'arg', 'Remote user (default: your name) and host.', true], ['[COMMAND]', 'arg', 'Run this instead of an interactive shell.', false]],
  ex1: 'ssh web01 hostname',
  sc: [['socket + connect', 'TCP connection to port 22', 'connect(3, {sin_port=htons(22), sin_addr=inet_addr("10.0.0.11")}, 16) = 0'], ['read/write (key exchange)', 'agree on encryption (Diffie-Hellman); verify the server host key against ~/.ssh/known_hosts', 'write(3, "SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_9.6p1 Ubuntu…", 41) = 41'], ['(authentication)', 'public key (signed with ~/.ssh/id_ed25519) or password', 'openat(AT_FDCWD, "/home/user/.ssh/id_ed25519", O_RDONLY) = 4'], ['(channel)', 'remote sshd starts your shell or command; stdin/stdout travel encrypted', '']],
  ch: 'Locally: ~/.ssh/known_hosts gets the server key on first connect. Remotely: whatever you run.', out: 'The remote shell/command output.', ec: 'The remote command\'s exit code; 255 = ssh connection error.',
  f: [['-p PORT', '', 'Non-standard port.', 'ssh -p 2222 user@host', true], ['-i KEY', '', 'Use a specific private key.', 'ssh -i ~/.ssh/work host', true], ['-v', '', 'Debug output (-vvv more).', 'ssh -v host', false], ['-L L:H:R', '', 'Local port forwarding (tunnel).', 'ssh -L 8080:localhost:80 host', false], ['-J JUMP', '', 'Go through a jump host.', 'ssh -J bastion web01', false], ['-t', '', 'Force a terminal (for interactive remote commands).', 'ssh -t host top', false]],
  e: [['ssh web01 hostname', 'web01', 'Run one command remotely.'], ['ssh user@db01 uptime', '', 'Specify the remote user.'], ['ssh web01', 'Welcome to Ubuntu …', 'Interactive login (simulated).'], ['ssh web02', 'ssh: connect to host web02 port 22: Connection timed out', 'Host is down.'], ['ssh-keygen -t ed25519', '', '(real Linux) create a key pair; then ssh-copy-id host.']],
  u: `<p><b>Keys beat passwords:</b> <code>ssh-keygen</code> creates a private key (stays on your machine, never share it) and a public key; <code>ssh-copy-id host</code> appends the public key to the server's <code>~/.ssh/authorized_keys</code>. Logging in then proves you hold the private key without sending any secret.</p><p>The <b>first connection</b> asks you to confirm the server's fingerprint and stores it in <code>~/.ssh/known_hosts</code>. A later “REMOTE HOST IDENTIFICATION HAS CHANGED” warning may mean a reinstall — or an attack. Shortcuts go in <code>~/.ssh/config</code>.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>SSH-2 transport: version exchange → KEXINIT → (curve25519) key exchange → NEWKEYS, then encrypted packets (chacha20-poly1305 or AES-GCM). User auth via “publickey” signs the session ID with your key. Channels multiplex shells, exec, port forwards and agent forwarding over one TCP connection. Server config: <code>/etc/ssh/sshd_config</code> (disable PasswordAuthentication, PermitRootLogin no).</p>`,
  mi: [['Ignoring “REMOTE HOST IDENTIFICATION HAS CHANGED”', 'Could be a man-in-the-middle.', 'Verify the new fingerprint out-of-band, then ssh-keygen -R host'], ['chmod 777 ~/.ssh', 'ssh refuses keys with loose permissions.', 'chmod 700 ~/.ssh; chmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_*'], ['ssh host top (garbled)', 'No terminal allocated for a remote interactive program.', 'ssh -t host top']],
  w: 'Protect your <b>private key</b> (chmod 600, use a passphrase) — anyone who copies it can log in as you. Disabling host key checking (<code>StrictHostKeyChecking no</code>) exposes you to man-in-the-middle attacks. On servers, disable password and root login once keys work.',
  r: [['scp', 'Copy files over SSH.'], ['rsync', 'Efficient sync over SSH.'], ['ufw', 'Allow port 22 BEFORE enabling a firewall on a remote server!']],
  cs: ['<code>ssh user@host</code> login', '<code>ssh host cmd</code> run one command', 'Keys: ssh-keygen + ssh-copy-id', 'known_hosts protects against impostors', 'Exit 255 = connection problem'],
  q: [['Which file on the SERVER lists keys allowed to log in?', ['~/.ssh/known_hosts', '~/.ssh/authorized_keys', '/etc/passwd', '~/.ssh/config'], 1, 'authorized_keys.'], ['What does known_hosts protect against?', ['Viruses', 'Connecting to an impostor server', 'Slow connections', 'Password reuse'], 1, 'It stores server fingerprints.'], ['Default SSH port?', ['21', '22', '80', '443'], 1, '22.']],
  c: { t: 'Run the command <code>hostname</code> on the server <code>web01</code> via SSH.', try: 'ssh web01 hostname', chk: ctx => okRun(ctx, 'ssh') && outOf(ctx).includes('web01') },
});
lesson('scp', {
  n: 'scp', s: 'Copies files between machines over SSH.',
  an: 'cp, but the photocopy is carried to another building in a locked briefcase.',
  nm: '<b>s</b>ecure <b>c</b>o<b>p</b>y.',
  sx: 'scp [-r] [-P PORT] SOURCE... TARGET   (remote = [user@]host:path)', p: [['scp', 'cmd', 'The command.', true], ['[-r] [-P PORT]', 'opt', '-r directories, -P port (capital!).', false], ['SOURCE...', 'arg', 'Local path or host:path.', true], ['TARGET', 'arg', 'Local path or host:path.', true]],
  ex1: 'scp notes.txt web01:/tmp/',
  sc: [['(ssh connection)', 'starts an SSH session (same handshake & auth as ssh)', 'execve("/usr/bin/ssh", ["ssh", "-x", "-oForwardAgent=no", …, "web01", "sftp"], …)'], ['openat + read', 'reads the local file', 'openat(AT_FDCWD, "notes.txt", O_RDONLY) = 3'], ['write (to ssh pipe)', 'sends the data encrypted over the channel', 'write(5, …, 104) = 104']],
  ch: 'Creates/overwrites the file at the target.', out: 'A progress line per file.', ec: '0 OK, 1 error, 255 connection error.',
  f: [['-r', '', 'Recursive (directories).', 'scp -r docs web01:', true], ['-P PORT', '', 'SSH port (capital P!).', 'scp -P 2222 f host:', true], ['-p', '', 'Preserve times and modes.', 'scp -p f host:', false], ['-i KEY', '', 'Identity file.', 'scp -i key f host:', false], ['-C', '', 'Compress in transit.', 'scp -C big.log host:', false], ['-q', '', 'Quiet.', 'scp -q f host:', false]],
  e: [['scp notes.txt web01:/tmp/', 'notes.txt   100%  104  …', 'Upload.'], ['scp web01:backup.tar.gz .', '', 'Download into the current directory.'], ['scp -r docs user@db01:~/', '', 'Upload a folder.'], ['scp f web02:', 'ssh: connect to host web02 port 22: Connection timed out', 'Host down.'], ['rsync -av docs/ web01:docs/', '', 'Often a better choice for folders.']],
  u: `<p>The colon makes the difference: <code>web01:file</code> is remote, <code>web01</code> alone would be a local file called “web01”! A path after the colon is relative to your remote home.</p><p>scp overwrites targets without asking and re-sends everything each time. For repeated or large transfers, <code>rsync</code> sends only differences and can resume.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>Since OpenSSH 9.0, scp uses the SFTP protocol under the hood by default (the legacy scp/rcp protocol had quoting vulnerabilities). Authentication, ciphers and ~/.ssh/config host aliases are shared with ssh.</p>`,
  mi: [['scp notes.txt web01', 'Creates a LOCAL copy named web01 (no colon!).', 'scp notes.txt web01:'], ['scp -p 2222 …', '-p preserves times; the port is -P.', 'scp -P 2222 f host:'], ['scp docs host:', 'not a regular file — needs -r.', 'scp -r docs host:']],
  w: 'scp <b>silently overwrites</b> files with the same name on the target. Double-check the destination path (especially with sudo on the remote side).',
  r: [['rsync', 'Incremental, resumable, can delete extras.'], ['ssh', 'Same authentication.'], ['cp', 'Local copies.']],
  cs: ['<code>scp f host:path</code> upload', '<code>scp host:path .</code> download', '<code>-r</code> folders · <code>-P</code> port', 'No colon = local file!', 'Repeated syncs → rsync'],
  q: [['What does scp notes.txt web01 do?', ['Uploads to web01', 'Copies to a local file named web01', 'Error', 'Opens SSH'], 1, 'Remote targets need a colon.'], ['Which option sets the port?', ['-p', '-P', '--port', '-o'], 1, 'Capital -P.'], ['What protects the data in transit?', ['Nothing', 'SSH encryption', 'gzip', 'HTTPS'], 1, 'scp runs over SSH.']],
  c: { t: 'Upload <code>fruits.txt</code> to the <code>/tmp/</code> directory on <code>web01</code>.', try: 'scp fruits.txt web01:/tmp/', chk: ctx => okRun(ctx, 'scp') && /web01:/.test(ctx.line) },
});

/* ======================= PACKAGES ======================= */
lesson('apt', {
  n: 'apt', s: 'Installs, updates and removes software packages on Debian/Ubuntu.',
  an: 'An app store with a catalogue: update refreshes the catalogue, install fetches an app (and everything it needs), remove takes it away.',
  nm: '<b>A</b>dvanced <b>P</b>ackage <b>T</b>ool.',
  sx: 'sudo apt update | install PKG... | remove PKG... | upgrade | search WORD | list --installed', p: [['apt', 'cmd', 'Package manager front-end (needs sudo to change anything).', true], ['update|install|remove|…', 'arg', 'The action.', true], ['PKG...', 'arg', 'Package names.', false]],
  ex1: 'sudo apt install tree',
  sc: [['openat lock', 'takes the dpkg lock — only one package operation at a time (needs root)', 'openat(AT_FDCWD, "/var/lib/dpkg/lock-frontend", O_RDWR|O_CREAT, 0640) = 3'], ['connect + recv', 'downloads .deb files from the repositories in /etc/apt/sources.list.d', 'connect(5, {sin_port=htons(80), …}) = 0'], ['(signature check)', 'verifies the repository signature (GPG) and the package checksum', ''], ['fork/execve dpkg', 'dpkg unpacks files into /usr/bin, /usr/share… and runs install scripts', 'execve("/usr/bin/dpkg", ["dpkg", "--unpack", …], …)']],
  ch: 'install: new files (e.g. /usr/bin/tree), dependency packages, package database. remove: deletes the program files (purge also config). update: refreshes the package lists in /var/lib/apt/lists.', out: 'Progress: Reading package lists… Get:… Setting up …', ec: '0 OK, 100 error (no root / unknown package / lock held).',
  f: [['update', '', 'Refresh the catalogue (doesn\'t install anything).', 'sudo apt update', true], ['upgrade', '', 'Upgrade installed packages.', 'sudo apt upgrade', true], ['install PKG', '', 'Install with dependencies.', 'sudo apt install htop', true], ['remove PKG', '', 'Remove (keep config files).', 'sudo apt remove htop', true], ['purge PKG', '', 'Remove including config.', 'sudo apt purge nginx', false], ['search WORD', '', 'Search the catalogue.', 'apt search json', false], ['show PKG', '', 'Details.', 'apt show tree', false], ['list --installed', '', 'Installed packages.', 'apt list --installed', false], ['-y', '', 'Assume yes.', 'sudo apt install -y jq', false], ['autoremove', '', 'Remove unneeded dependencies.', 'sudo apt autoremove', false]],
  e: [['apt install tree', 'E: Could not open lock file … are you root?', 'Changing the system needs root.'], ['sudo apt update', 'Hit:1 http://archive.ubuntu.com …', 'Refresh lists.'], ['sudo apt install tree', 'Setting up tree (2.1.1-2) …', 'Install.'], ['tree docs', 'docs\n├── readme.txt …', 'The new command works.'], ['apt search json', '', 'Find packages.'], ['sudo apt remove cowsay', '', 'Uninstall.']],
  u: `<p>A <b>package</b> (.deb) is an archive of files + metadata: version, description, <b>dependencies</b>, and install/remove scripts. apt downloads packages from <b>repositories</b> (listed in /etc/apt/sources.list.d/), verifies their GPG signatures, resolves dependencies, then calls <code>dpkg</code> to install.</p><p><b>update vs upgrade:</b> <code>update</code> only refreshes the list of available versions; <code>upgrade</code> actually installs newer versions. Typical: <code>sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade</code>.</p><p>“Command 'x' not found, but can be installed with…” is Ubuntu's command-not-found helper looking up which package provides it.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>apt (libapt-pkg) keeps lists in <code>/var/lib/apt/lists</code>, downloaded .debs in <code>/var/cache/apt/archives</code>, and the installed-package database in <code>/var/lib/dpkg/status</code>. A .deb is an ar archive containing control.tar (metadata, maintainer scripts preinst/postinst) and data.tar (files). <code>dpkg -L pkg</code> lists installed files; <code>dpkg -S /usr/bin/tree</code> finds the owner package.</p>`,
  mi: [['apt install x without sudo', 'Lock error (exit 100).', 'sudo apt install x'], ['Only running apt update and expecting new versions', 'update just refreshes the list.', 'sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade'], ['Killing apt mid-install', 'Leaves dpkg locked / half-configured.', 'sudo dpkg --configure -a'], ['Installing random .deb files from the web', 'Runs their install scripts as root.', 'Prefer official repositories']],
  w: 'Package operations run maintainer scripts <b>as root</b>. Only add repositories/PPAs and .deb files you trust. <code>apt remove</code> of core packages (python3, systemd…) can remove half the system along with dependents — read the list apt prints before answering “Y”.',
  r: [['sudo', 'Required for install/remove.'], ['which', 'Check where the new program landed.'], ['dpkg', 'Low-level tool apt uses underneath.']],
  cs: ['<code>sudo apt update</code> refresh catalogue', '<code>sudo apt upgrade</code> install updates', '<code>sudo apt install pkg</code>', '<code>sudo apt remove pkg</code> (<code>purge</code> = + config)', '<code>apt search</code> / <code>apt show</code>'],
  q: [['What does apt update do?', ['Upgrades all packages', 'Refreshes the package lists', 'Updates apt itself only', 'Reboots'], 1, 'Use upgrade to install new versions.'], ['Why does apt install fail without sudo?', ['Network', 'It needs root to lock dpkg and write system files', 'Package missing', 'Bug'], 1, 'Exit code 100.'], ['remove vs purge?', ['Same', 'purge also deletes config files', 'remove deletes config', 'purge is faster'], 1, 'purge = remove + config.']],
  c: { t: 'Install the <code>tree</code> package and use it to view your docs folder.', try: 'sudo apt install tree', steps: [['Install tree with sudo apt install', ctx => S.pkgs.installed.has('tree')], ['Run tree on a folder', ctx => okRun(ctx, 'tree')]] },
});

/* ======================= USERS & GROUPS ======================= */
lesson('useradd', {
  n: 'useradd', s: 'Creates a new user account (root only).',
  an: 'Registering a new employee: they get an ID number, a personal locker (home directory) and a badge for certain departments (groups).',
  nm: '<b>user</b> <b>add</b>.',
  sx: 'sudo useradd [-m] [-s SHELL] [-G GROUPS] [-d HOME] NAME', p: [['useradd', 'cmd', 'Low-level account creation tool (/usr/sbin).', true], ['[OPTIONS]', 'opt', '-m create home, -s login shell, -G extra groups, -c comment.', false], ['NAME', 'arg', 'Login name (lowercase).', true]],
  ex1: 'sudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash bob',
  sc: [['openat /etc/passwd (lock)', 'locks and edits the account databases', 'openat(AT_FDCWD, "/etc/passwd.lock", O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_EXCL, 0600) = 3'], ['write /etc/passwd, /etc/group, /etc/shadow', 'adds lines for the new user and their private group', 'write(4, "bob:x:1002:1003::/home/bob:/bin/bash\\n", 38) = 38'], ['mkdir + copy /etc/skel', 'with -m: creates the home directory and copies default dotfiles', 'mkdir("/home/bob", 0700) = 0'], ['chown', 'gives the home to the new user', 'chown("/home/bob", 1002, 1003) = 0']],
  ch: 'New lines in /etc/passwd, /etc/shadow, /etc/group; optionally /home/NAME.', out: 'Nothing.', ec: '0 OK, 1 can\'t update password file, 9 username exists.',
  f: [['-m', '--create-home', 'Create the home directory (NOT default on Debian/Ubuntu).', 'useradd -m bob', true], ['-s SHELL', '--shell', 'Login shell (default /bin/sh).', '-s /bin/bash', true], ['-G G1,G2', '--groups', 'Supplementary groups.', '-G developers,docker', true], ['-d DIR', '--home-dir', 'Home path.', '-d /srv/bob', false], ['-c TEXT', '--comment', 'Full name / description.', '-c "Bob Smith"', false], ['-r', '--system', 'System account (low UID, no home).', 'useradd -r svc', false], ['usermod -aG', '', '(related) Add an existing user to a group.', 'usermod -aG docker bob', true]],
  e: [['useradd bob', 'useradd: Permission denied.', 'Needs root.'], ['sudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash bob', '', 'Proper user with a home and bash.'], ['id bob', 'uid=1002(bob) gid=1003(bob) groups=1003(bob)', 'Check.'], ['sudo usermod -aG developers bob; groups bob', 'bob : bob developers', 'Add to a group.'], ['sudo passwd bob', '', 'Set a password so bob can log in.'], ['grep bob /etc/passwd', 'bob:x:1002:1003::/home/bob:/bin/bash', 'The new entry.']],
  u: `<p><b>/etc/passwd</b> fields: <code>name:x:UID:GID:comment:home:shell</code> — readable by all. The “x” means the password hash is in <b>/etc/shadow</b>, readable only by root. <b>/etc/group</b>: <code>group:x:GID:members</code>.</p><p>On Debian/Ubuntu, <code>adduser</code> is a friendlier interactive wrapper that creates the home and asks for a password; <code>useradd</code> is the low-level tool with distro-independent behaviour (no home without -m!).</p>`,
  adv: `<p>Defaults come from <code>/etc/default/useradd</code> and <code>/etc/login.defs</code> (UID_MIN, UMASK, USERGROUPS_ENAB → per-user private group). Files are updated atomically via lock files and rename. New users get no password (“!” in shadow) → can't log in by password until <code>passwd</code>.</p>`,
  mi: [['useradd bob (on Ubuntu)', 'No home directory and /bin/sh as shell.', 'sudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash bob   (or: sudo adduser bob)'], ['usermod -G docker bob', 'REPLACES all of bob\'s groups with docker!', 'usermod -aG docker bob'], ['Expecting new groups to work in the current session', 'Group changes apply at next login.', 'Log out/in (or newgrp)']],
  w: '<b>usermod -G without -a</b> removes the user from every group not listed — e.g. kicking yourself out of <code>sudo</code>. Never hand-edit /etc/passwd or /etc/shadow with a normal editor; use useradd/usermod or <code>vipw</code>.',
  r: [['passwd', 'Give the new account a password.'], ['groups', 'Check memberships.'], ['su', 'Test logging in as the new user.']],
  cs: ['<code>sudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash bob</code>', '<code>sudo passwd bob</code> set password', '<code>sudo usermod -aG grp bob</code> (keep -a!)', 'Accounts: /etc/passwd · hashes: /etc/shadow', 'Ubuntu friendly: adduser'],
  q: [['Which option creates the home directory?', ['-h', '-m', '-d', '-c'], 1, '-m = create home.'], ['Where are password hashes stored?', ['/etc/passwd', '/etc/shadow', '/etc/group', '~/.password'], 1, 'Root-only /etc/shadow.'], ['What does usermod -G docker bob (without -a) do?', ['Adds docker', 'Replaces all supplementary groups with docker', 'Nothing', 'Deletes bob'], 1, 'Always use -aG to append.']],
  c: { t: 'Create a user <code>bob</code> with a home directory and bash as login shell.', try: 'sudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash bob', chk: ctx => S.users.bob && has('/home/bob') && S.users.bob.shell === '/bin/bash' },
});
lesson('passwd', {
  n: 'passwd', s: 'Changes a user\'s password (your own, or anyone\'s as root).',
  an: 'Changing the combination of your own locker. The building manager (root) can reset anyone\'s combination.',
  nm: '<b>passw</b>or<b>d</b>.',
  sx: 'passwd [OPTIONS] [USER]', p: [['passwd', 'cmd', 'setuid-root program.', true], ['[OPTIONS]', 'opt', '-l lock, -u unlock, -S status, -e expire.', false], ['[USER]', 'arg', 'Whose password (root only for others).', false]],
  ex1: 'passwd',
  sc: [['execve (setuid)', '/usr/bin/passwd is -rwsr-xr-x root — it runs as root so it can write /etc/shadow', 'execve("/usr/bin/passwd", ["passwd"], …) = 0'], ['(PAM)', 'asks for the current password and checks strength rules', ''], ['openat /etc/shadow', 'writes the new salted hash (yescrypt/SHA-512)', 'openat(AT_FDCWD, "/etc/nshadow", O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_TRUNC, 0640) = 4'], ['rename', 'atomically replaces /etc/shadow', 'rename("/etc/nshadow", "/etc/shadow") = 0']],
  ch: 'Only the user\'s line in /etc/shadow (the hash and change date).', out: 'Prompts (typing is invisible), then “password updated successfully”.', ec: '0 OK, non-zero on failure.',
  f: [['USER', '', 'Change another user\'s password (root).', 'sudo passwd bob', true], ['-l', '--lock', 'Lock the account\'s password.', 'sudo passwd -l bob', true], ['-u', '--unlock', 'Unlock.', 'sudo passwd -u bob', false], ['-S', '--status', 'Password status.', 'passwd -S', false], ['-e', '--expire', 'Force change at next login.', 'sudo passwd -e bob', false], ['-d', '--delete', 'Remove the password (dangerous).', 'sudo passwd -d bob', false]],
  e: [['passwd', 'Changing password for user.\nCurrent password: …', 'Your own password.'], ['passwd alice', 'passwd: You may not view or modify password information for alice.', 'Others need root.'], ['sudo passwd alice', '', 'Root can reset it (no old password needed).'], ['ls -l /usr/bin/passwd', '-rwsr-xr-x 1 root root … /usr/bin/passwd', 'The s = setuid: why it can write /etc/shadow.'], ['sudo passwd -l bob', '', 'Lock an account.']],
  u: `<p>Linux never stores passwords — only <b>salted hashes</b> in /etc/shadow (format <code>$y$...</code> = yescrypt, <code>$6$</code> = SHA-512). When you log in, your input is hashed with the same salt and compared. The salt makes identical passwords produce different hashes.</p><p>Since /etc/shadow is root-only, <code>passwd</code> is <b>setuid root</b> — a classic example of why that bit exists. It only lets you change YOUR line unless you're really root.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>passwd uses PAM (<code>/etc/pam.d/passwd</code>, common-password with pam_unix/pam_pwquality). Shadow fields: name:hash:lastchg:min:max:warn:inactive:expire. “!” prefix = locked, “*” = no password login. Change aging with <code>chage</code>.</p>`,
  mi: [['Typing and seeing nothing → thinking it froze', 'Password input is intentionally invisible.', 'Just type and press Enter'], ['passwd -d user', 'Account without password — may allow login without any secret.', 'passwd -l user'], ['Weak password because “it\'s just a test VM”', 'Exposed VMs get brute-forced within minutes.', 'Use SSH keys; long passphrases']],
  safe: 'Only changes a password hash. (Locking/deleting passwords can lock people out.)',
  r: [['useradd', 'Create the account first.'], ['setuid', 'The bit that makes passwd work.'], ['su', 'Uses the target\'s password.']],
  cs: ['<code>passwd</code> change yours', '<code>sudo passwd bob</code> reset another', 'Hashes in /etc/shadow (root only)', 'passwd is setuid root', '<code>-l</code> lock · <code>-S</code> status'],
  q: [['Where are password hashes?', ['/etc/passwd', '/etc/shadow', '/var/passwd', 'nowhere'], 1, '/etc/shadow.'], ['How can passwd write a root-only file?', ['It asks root', 'It is setuid root', 'Through sudo', 'It doesn\'t'], 1, 'The s bit on /usr/bin/passwd.'], ['Can a user change someone else\'s password?', ['Yes', 'Only with sudo/root', 'If in same group', 'Only on weekends'], 1, 'Root only.']],
  c: { t: 'Look at the permissions of <code>/usr/bin/passwd</code> and spot the special <b>s</b> bit, then change your own password.', try: 'ls -l /usr/bin/passwd', steps: [['ls -l /usr/bin/passwd', ctx => okRun(ctx, 'ls') && ctx.line.includes('/usr/bin/passwd')], ['Run passwd', ctx => okRun(ctx, 'passwd')]] },
});
lesson('groups', {
  n: 'groups', s: 'Shows which groups a user belongs to.',
  an: 'Listing the department badges on your key ring: each one opens the doors of that department.',
  nm: '<b>groups</b>.',
  sx: 'groups [USER]...', p: [['groups', 'cmd', 'The command.', true], ['[USER]...', 'arg', 'Users to check (default: you).', false]],
  ex1: 'groups',
  sc: [['getgroups', 'asks the kernel for the supplementary groups of this process', 'getgroups(65536, [4, 27, 999, 1000, 1002]) = 5'], ['read /etc/group', 'translates numbers to names', 'openat(AT_FDCWD, "/etc/group", O_RDONLY|O_CLOEXEC) = 3']],
  ch: 'Nothing.', out: 'Space-separated group names (with USER: “user : groups…”).', ec: '0, 1 if a user doesn\'t exist.',
  f: [['USER', '', 'Another user\'s groups.', 'groups alice', true], ['id -Gn', '', '(alternative) same list.', 'id -Gn', false], ['newgrp G', '', '(related) Start a shell with group G active.', 'newgrp docker', false]],
  e: [['groups', 'user adm sudo docker developers', 'Your groups.'], ['groups alice', 'alice : alice developers', 'Someone else\'s.'], ['id', 'uid=1000(user) gid=1000(user) groups=…', 'With numbers.'], ['grep developers /etc/group', 'developers:x:1002:user,alice', 'The source of truth.'], ['ls -l /var/log/syslog', '-rw-r----- 1 root adm …', 'Group adm may read it — and you are in adm.']],
  u: `<p>Every user has one <b>primary group</b> (new files get it) and any number of <b>supplementary groups</b>. Groups are how you share access: put users in <code>developers</code>, give a folder group <code>developers</code> and mode 2770. Special groups: <code>sudo</code> (may use sudo), <code>adm</code> (read logs), <code>docker</code> (control Docker — effectively root!).</p><p>Group membership is read <b>at login</b>; after <code>usermod -aG</code> you must log out and in again.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>The kernel stores supplementary GIDs in <code>cred->group_info</code> (max NGROUPS_MAX = 65536) set by <code>setgroups()</code> during login (initgroups from /etc/group/NSS). Permission checks use these numbers; renaming a group doesn't change file access.</p>`,
  mi: [['Added to a group but still “permission denied”', 'The current session has the old group list.', 'Log out and back in'], ['Adding users to docker casually', 'docker group = root-equivalent.', 'Grant only to admins'], ['Confusing groups with $GROUPS', '$GROUPS is a bash array of GIDs.', 'groups']],
  safe: 'Read-only.',
  r: [['useradd', 'usermod -aG to add members.'], ['chown', 'chgrp to give a file to a group.'], ['whoami', 'Your user name.']],
  cs: ['<code>groups</code> your groups', '<code>groups alice</code>', 'sudo / adm / docker are special', 'Changes apply at next login', 'Source: /etc/group'],
  q: [['When do new group memberships take effect?', ['Immediately', 'At next login', 'After reboot only', 'Never'], 1, 'Log out and in.'], ['Which group lets users run sudo on Ubuntu?', ['wheel', 'sudo', 'adm', 'root'], 1, '%sudo in /etc/sudoers.'], ['Where is group membership defined?', ['/etc/group', '/etc/passwd only', '/proc/groups', '~/.groups'], 0, '/etc/group.']],
  c: { t: 'Show your groups and alice\'s groups — which group do you share?', try: 'groups', steps: [['groups', ctx => okRun(ctx, 'groups') && !/alice/.test(ctx.line)], ['groups alice', ctx => okRun(ctx, 'groups') && ctx.line.includes('alice')]] },
});
lesson('su', {
  n: 'su', s: 'Switches to another user (by default root) in a new shell.',
  an: 'Borrowing someone\'s keys after proving you know their PIN — you can then act as them until you hand the keys back (exit).',
  nm: '<b>s</b>ubstitute <b>u</b>ser (or switch user).',
  sx: 'su [-] [USER] [-c COMMAND]', p: [['su', 'cmd', 'setuid-root program.', true], ['[-]', 'opt', 'Login shell: reset environment and go to the target\'s home.', false], ['[USER]', 'arg', 'Target user (default root).', false], ['[-c COMMAND]', 'opt', 'Run one command and return.', false]],
  ex1: 'su - alice',
  sc: [['execve (setuid)', 'su starts with root privileges', 'execve("/usr/bin/su", ["su", "-", "alice"], …) = 0'], ['(PAM)', 'asks for the TARGET user\'s password', ''], ['setgid / setgroups / setuid', 'becomes the target user', 'setuid(1001) = 0'], ['execve shell', 'starts the target\'s login shell', 'execve("/bin/bash", ["-bash"], …) = 0']],
  ch: 'Starts a new shell as the target user (nested inside yours). exit returns.', out: 'Password prompt; then a new prompt as that user.', ec: '0, or 1 on authentication failure.',
  f: [['-', '-l, --login', 'Full login: fresh environment, cd to home.', 'su - alice', true], ['-c CMD', '--command', 'Run one command.', 'su - alice -c whoami', true], ['USER', '', 'Who to become.', 'su alice', true], ['-s SHELL', '', 'Use a specific shell.', 'su -s /bin/sh svc', false]],
  e: [['su - alice -c whoami', 'alice', 'Run one command as alice.'], ['su - alice', '', 'Become alice (prompt changes) — exit to return.'], ['exit', 'logout', 'Back to yourself.'], ['sudo -i', '', 'On Ubuntu use this for a root shell (root has no password for su).'], ['sudo su - alice', '', 'Become alice WITHOUT her password, via your sudo rights.']],
  u: `<p><b>su vs sudo:</b> su asks for the <b>target's</b> password and gives you a whole shell; sudo asks for <b>your</b> password, checks /etc/sudoers, logs each command, and runs just one command. Ubuntu locks the root password, so <code>su</code> to root fails there — <code>sudo -i</code> is the equivalent.</p><p>Always prefer <code>su -</code> (with the dash): without it you keep YOUR environment (PATH, HOME…), which causes confusing behaviour.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>su authenticates via PAM (<code>/etc/pam.d/su</code>; pam_wheel can restrict su to a group). It then calls initgroups/setgid/setuid and execs the shell with argv[0] = "-bash" (leading dash → login shell reads /etc/profile and ~/.profile). The new shell is a child of the old one — <code>ps --forest</code> shows the nesting.</p>`,
  mi: [['su (on Ubuntu) → “Authentication failure”', 'root has no password.', 'sudo -i'], ['su alice (without -)', 'Keeps your PATH/HOME — confusing.', 'su - alice'], ['Staying root after finishing', 'Every later typo has root power.', 'exit as soon as you\'re done']],
  w: 'A root shell (<code>su -</code> / <code>sudo -i</code>) removes all safety nets for <b>every</b> command until you exit — and individual commands are no longer logged like with sudo. Use it briefly, watch for the <code>#</code> prompt, and exit.',
  r: [['sudo', 'One command, your password, logged — preferred.'], ['whoami', 'Check who you are now.'], ['passwd', 'Set the password su will ask for.']],
  cs: ['<code>su - user</code> become user (login shell)', '<code>su - user -c cmd</code> one command', 'Asks the TARGET\'s password', '<code>exit</code> returns', 'Ubuntu root shell: <code>sudo -i</code>'],
  q: [['Whose password does su ask for?', ['Yours', 'The target user\'s', 'None', 'root\'s always'], 1, 'Unlike sudo.'], ['What does the - in su - do?', ['Nothing', 'Starts a login shell with a fresh environment', 'Quiet mode', 'Removes the user'], 1, 'Full login environment.'], ['How do you return to your own user?', ['logout only works for root', 'exit', 'su -', 'Ctrl+Z'], 1, 'exit ends the nested shell.']],
  c: { t: 'Run <code>whoami</code> as <b>alice</b> using su.', try: 'su - alice -c whoami', chk: ctx => ran(ctx, 'su') && outOf(ctx).includes('alice') },
});
